  "interpretation": [
    "For a shared pixel covered by both meshes, complementary thresholds choose exactly one LOD. The matching-geometry native fixture checks this property on Mobile and Compatibility.",
    "Pixels present in only one endpoint appear/disappear gradually with the dither fraction. In expectation area is (1-t)*A+t*B; geometry differences and differing shading remain. This is a mathematical interpretation, not a measured motion acceptance test.",
    "Near live crowns lose 11.6\u201316.2% of their old foreground positions while acquiring new ones; total area stays within about 5%, so most failure is pattern redistribution rather than a disappearing crown.",
    "At 65 m, live crowns gain 15.4\u201329.3% endpoint foreground area. A gradual density change can remain visible while walking; complementary dither does not repair it.",
    "At 180 m the snag pair may differ by 16\u201322 pixels, with only 6\u201322 foreground pixels per endpoint; this explains numerical sensitivity but does not waive the failing limit.",
    "This audit is for fixed azimuths and wind-frozen transparent captures. It does not certify full-world motion, all camera angles, lighting equality or frame time.",
    "Far impostors are visibly darker in the inspected fixed-lighting endpoint images: median pair luminance ratio 0.441, range 0.333\u20130.706. The 36 m blend interval spreads this change, but does not correct baked versus dynamic lighting differences. Actual-world lighting acceptance is separate."
  ],
  "release_wording": "Tree LOD changes now blend per tree instead of switching an entire batch at once. The final raw endpoint comparison still passes 34 of 96 cases: near live-crown area stays within about 5%, but branch-gap patterns change; middle LOD crowns become 15\u201329% fuller through the 58.5\u201371.5 m blend. Distant live trees pass 18 of 24 cases; the largest relative snag discrepancies involve only 6\u201322 visible pixels. The blend removes a hard boundary switch but does not certify identical silhouettes or invisible transitions. Far billboard brightness can also change through the transition; the fixed-lighting comparison does not establish a lighting match.",
